# -- end of source
# f2 = run, with blend() copied in. Both lists are tested in front of the loop for lists of numbers. In `a[0] * b[0]` the left element may read a hole as NaN, because a product makes NaN of
# undefined. In `a[0] + b[0]` it may do so only where the right side is no text: it is an element off a list of numbers tested in front too, and nothing but constants and casts stands between
# the two reads. All six reads are then raw doubles known to be present; none gives `undefined|number`. runHoled: a hole on the left gives NaN. runNamed: beside a text it gives "undefinedx".
# RUN: walk f2 mechanisms=all
# CHECK: number @Double = GetArrayElement {{*}} arrayStorage=Double {{*}} isPresent
# CHECK: number @Double = GetArrayElement {{*}} arrayStorage=Double {{*}} isPresent
# CHECK: number @Double = GetArrayElement {{*}} arrayStorage=Double {{*}} isPresent
# CHECK: number @Double = GetArrayElement {{*}} arrayStorage=Double {{*}} isPresent
# CHECK: number @Double = GetArrayElement {{*}} arrayStorage=Double {{*}} isPresent
# CHECK: number @Double = GetArrayElement {{*}} arrayStorage=Double {{*}} isPresent
# RUN(NONE): walk f2 mechanisms=all
# NONE-NOT: undefined|number @OptionalDouble = GetArrayElement {{*}} arrayStorage=Double
# RUN(ANSWER): run-in-shell mechanisms=all
